From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henk Wiedig <henk@wiedig.de>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 19:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] watchdog: dw_wdt: restart with a timeout of a second

The restart handler armed the reset with the shortest TOP, 2^16 cycles
(2.7 ms at 24 MHz). On the Artosyn AR9311 the SoC then nearly never came
back from a `reboot`: no boot until a power cycle. Armed with a TOP of
1.4 s, it came back each time, also with every CPU stopped and IRQs off
as here, so it is the length of the timeout and not the state the
restart path leaves. Measured on a BetaFPV P1 air unit (the same SoC and
kernel; OpenIPC/builder's ml-kernel patch 0006 is this one): 0 out of 9
warm resets with TOP 0, 7 out of 7 with 1.4 s. The restart now takes the
shortest TOP of at least a second and waits until it expires.

---
--- a/drivers/watchdog/dw_wdt.c
+++ b/drivers/watchdog/dw_wdt.c
@@ -301,8 +301,19 @@
 			  unsigned long action, void *data)
 {
 	struct dw_wdt *dw_wdt = to_dw_wdt(wdd);
+	unsigned int timeout;
+	u32 top_val;
 
-	writel(0, dw_wdt->regs + WDOG_TIMEOUT_RANGE_REG_OFFSET);
+	/*
+	 * Reset after a TOP of at least a second, not the shortest one. On the
+	 * Artosyn AR9311 a reset armed with TOP 0 (2^16 cycles, 2.7 ms at
+	 * 24 MHz) leaves the SoC hung until a power cycle nearly every time,
+	 * while one armed with a TOP of 1.4 s comes back every time, also from
+	 * here with the other CPUs stopped.
+	 */
+	timeout = dw_wdt_find_best_top(dw_wdt, 1, &top_val);
+	writel(top_val | top_val << WDOG_TIMEOUT_RANGE_TOPINIT_SHIFT,
+	       dw_wdt->regs + WDOG_TIMEOUT_RANGE_REG_OFFSET);
 	dw_wdt_update_mode(dw_wdt, DW_WDT_RMOD_RESET);
 	if (dw_wdt_is_enabled(dw_wdt))
 		writel(WDOG_COUNTER_RESTART_KICK_VALUE,
@@ -310,8 +321,8 @@
 	else
 		dw_wdt_arm_system_reset(dw_wdt);
 
-	/* wait for reset to assert... */
-	mdelay(500);
+	/* wait for reset to assert: the TOP is shorter than timeout + 1 s */
+	mdelay((timeout + 1) * 1000);
 
 	return 0;
 }
